        "2": {
            "name": "text_block",
            "id": 311,
            "position_x": 862,
            "position_y": 45,
            "width": 768,
            "height": 776,
            "visibility": "show_all",
            "draggable": true,
            "presentation_state": "show_all",
            "properties": {
                "0": {
                    "name": "block",
                    "value": "Adds up the values along a matrix's diagonal, from the top left corner down to the right.\n\nFor a covariance matrix it is the total variance - how much the data varies altogether, in every direction combined. For a rotation matrix it gives the angle of rotation (the trace is 1 + 2 cos of the angle), so it measures how far one orientation is from another without needing the axis. For a correlation matrix it is simply the number of variables.\n\nSYNTAX:\nnp.trace <diagonal: int>\nDefault: 0, the main diagonal.\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nin:\nA matrix (or a stack of them: the last two axes are used, one trace each). Every input is summed and sent.\n\ndiagonal (int box, also an inlet):\nWhich diagonal: 0 the main one, 1 the one above it, -1 the one below.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\ntrace:\nThe sum.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\n1 to 9 as a 3 x 3 matrix. Its diagonal is 1 5 9, so the trace is 15; the diagonal just above it is 2 6, which sums to 8. A metro re-sends the matrix five times a second.\n\nRELATED:\nnp.diag reads the diagonal off as a list. np.sum adds everything. np.cov's trace is its total variance.",
                    "value_type": "str"
                },
                "1": {
                    "name": "lock",
                    "value": true,
                    "value_type": "bool"
                },
                "2": {
                    "name": "width",
                    "value": 760,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "3": {
                    "name": "height",
                    "value": 760,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "4": {
                    "name": "text_size",
                    "value": "30",
                    "value_type": "str"
                }
            },
            "text": "Adds up the values along a matrix's diagonal, from the top left corner down to the right.\n\nFor a covariance matrix it is the total variance - how much the data varies altogether, in every direction combined. For a rotation matrix it gives the angle of rotation (the trace is 1 + 2 cos of the angle), so it measures how far one orientation is from another without needing the axis. For a correlation matrix it is simply the number of variables.\n\nSYNTAX:\nnp.trace <diagonal: int>\nDefault: 0, the main diagonal.\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nin:\nA matrix (or a stack of them: the last two axes are used, one trace each). Every input is summed and sent.\n\ndiagonal (int box, also an inlet):\nWhich diagonal: 0 the main one, 1 the one above it, -1 the one below.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\ntrace:\nThe sum.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\n1 to 9 as a 3 x 3 matrix. Its diagonal is 1 5 9, so the trace is 15; the diagonal just above it is 2 6, which sums to 8. A metro re-sends the matrix five times a second.\n\nRELATED:\nnp.diag reads the diagonal off as a list. np.sum adds everything. np.cov's trace is its total variance."
        },
